A checkbox is a good case study specifically because a native `<input type="checkbox">` almost always beats a custom one — but when a design system genuinely needs full visual control a native input can't provide, this is what a correct from-scratch implementation looks like:

```html
<div
  role="checkbox"
  tabindex="0"
  aria-checked="false"
  aria-labelledby="opt-label"
>
</div>
<span id="opt-label">Email me updates</span>
```
```js
el.addEventListener('click', toggle);
el.addEventListener('keydown', e => {
  if (e.key === ' ' || e.key === 'Enter') { e.preventDefault(); toggle(); }
});
function toggle() {
  const checked = el.getAttribute('aria-checked') === 'true';
  el.setAttribute('aria-checked', String(!checked)); // the state that must never drift
}
```

Every piece here — the role, the initial state, the label association, both the click and keyboard handlers, and updating `aria-checked` on every toggle — is something `<input type="checkbox">` provides natively. This is the concrete cost of choosing custom-plus-ARIA over native.
